Take a look at our “big N” (i.e. population counts) rows:



The `by` argument was not supplied and cannot reliably be inferred from objects
of class &lt;bind_ard&gt;.
• A `by` value of "ARM2" was found in the input object's attributes.
• To use it as a grouping variable, pass it explicitly: `shuffle_card(by =
  "ARM2")`.


# A tibble: 6 × 8
  ARM2   variable_level context stat_variable stat_name stat_label    stat label
  &lt;chr&gt;  &lt;chr&gt;          &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;         &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt;
1 Place… &lt;NA&gt;           tabula… ARM2          n         n           86     &lt;NA&gt; 
2 Place… &lt;NA&gt;           tabula… ARM2          N         N          254     &lt;NA&gt; 
3 Place… &lt;NA&gt;           tabula… ARM2          p         %            0.339 &lt;NA&gt; 
4 Xanom… &lt;NA&gt;           tabula… ARM2          n         n          168     &lt;NA&gt; 
5 Xanom… &lt;NA&gt;           tabula… ARM2          N         N          254     &lt;NA&gt; 
6 Xanom… &lt;NA&gt;           tabula… ARM2          p         %            0.661 &lt;NA&gt; 




First, we only need the counts themselves (‘n’), not denominators or percentages (‘N’, ‘%’) to display in the column headers.
Second, we need to give these a unique stat name to distinguish them for the big_n_structure in {tfrmt}.

